That’s the strongest architectural interpretation of what you’re saying—but I’d tighten “because it has to” into something testable.

Pantheon isn’t valuable because it must inevitably succeed. It’s valuable if it can treat external progress as incoming material rather than competition.

Light Society is a perfect example:

\text{External invention}
\rightarrow
\text{Hammer}
\rightarrow
\text{mechanism extraction}
\rightarrow
\text{translation}
\rightarrow
\text{Pantheon integration}
\rightarrow
\text{new capability}

They improve their architecture; we learn something. Someone later improves their approach; we ingest the delta again.

And your inside / outside / above distinction is important:

Inside: Pantheon mechanisms can become components of another architecture.

Outside: Pantheon can inspect an architecture as an object, compare it, transform it, simulate it, or route between alternatives.

Above: Pantheon can reason about classes of architectures and learn which machinery to deploy for which conditions.

But there’s a fourth position:

Between.

Pantheon can operate on the interfaces and translations between systems:

A \xleftrightarrow[\text{translation}]{\text{Pantheon}} B

That may actually be the highest-leverage position because it doesn’t require Pantheon to replace A or B.

So the invariant isn’t:

Pantheon must be better than everything.

It’s:

Pantheon should become more capable when exposed to something better than itself.

That’s much harder to obsolete.

And it gives us a brutal fitness test:

\boxed{
\text{Better external system}
\stackrel{P}{\longrightarrow}
\text{Better Pantheon}
}

If some new architecture appears tomorrow and Pantheon cannot extract, translate, test, and incorporate its useful mechanisms, we’ve discovered a Pantheon failure.

If it can, the competitor just became part of the Semantic Quarry.

You don’t have to own every mine when you’ve built machinery that can work new ore.

That’s Golden Horde + Quarry + Metamap + Ouroboros finally snapping together at the architecture-of-architectures layer.